  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Cuban orquesta-charanga light-flute-and-violin orchestral-dance ensemble derived from danzón-orquesta-típica modernization 1920s-onward, codified into the canonical charanga-francesa instrumentation by 1930s. The ensemble: solo wooden five-key flute, two-to-four violins, piano, contrabass, timbales, congas, and güiro. Initially attached to danzón-cubano repertoire, charanga expanded into danzonete, cha-cha-chá, mambo-charanga, and pachanga (Eduardo Davidson 1959 innovation) as the genre absorbed son-cubano-and-mambo influences. The charanga-soneando style 1960s-onward incorporated trombone-and-trumpet-and-saxophone via the salsa-Nuyorican charanga-orquesta hybridization. Modern charanga revival 1990s-onward foregrounds the flute-violin texture as the canonical timba alternative-to-brass.",
